<Base title="Pressure washer spray gun inlet" description="Identify the inlet on a pressure-washer gun: M22-14, M22-15, or 3/8 inch quick-connect. The wand outlet is a second fitting." path="/spray-gun/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Spray gun</p>
    <h1>Spray-gun inlets</h1>
    <Answer>
      The handle inlet is one fitting. The wand outlet is another. A “universal gun” that lists M22 may still be the wrong stem.
    </Answer>
    <p>When a new gun leaks at the hose:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Confirm the hose end with <a href="/measure-m22/">a stem measurement</a>.</li>
      <li>Confirm the gun inlet the same way.</li>
      <li>If they match and it still weeps, replace the inlet O-ring before you return the gun.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Aftermarket “short trigger” guns are often M22-14 mm. That is why they leak on a <a href="/brands/sun-joe/">Sun Joe</a> hose until you add the 15 mm coupler.</p>
    <p>If you are converting the whole kit, put <a href="/qc-38/">3/8″ QC</a> at this inlet and stop buying a new gun every time the hose changes.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
